<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Samsung Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsung is a South Korean business group founded in 1938 by Lee Byung-chul as a trading company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daegu">Daegu</a>. Its largest member, Samsung Electronics, became one of the world's biggest makers of smartphones, memory chips and televisions. From 2008 Samsung built large phone factories in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, becoming the country's largest foreign investor and a major source of its exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsung.jpg" alt="Samsung" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oskar Alexanderson · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsung_headquarters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to its founder, the name Samsung means three stars: the three stands for something big, numerous and powerful, and the stars for something everlasting. In 1938, in the Japanese era, Lee Byung-chul, a member of a large landowning family, moved to nearby Taikyu and founded the Mitsuboshi Trading Company, in Korean Samsung Sanghoe. It began as a small trading company of forty employees, dealing in dried fish, locally grown groceries and noodles. It prospered, and Lee moved its head office to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> in 1947. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> broke out he was forced to leave Seoul and started a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> refinery in Pusan named Cheil Jedang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1954 Lee founded the textiles company Cheil Mojik and built its first plant in Taegu, the largest woollen mill in the country at the time. Over the next decades the group diversified into food processing, textiles, insurance, securities and retail. It entered the electronics industry in the late 1960s, and the construction and shipbuilding sectors in the mid-1970s. In 1980 it acquired Hanguk Jeonja Tongsin, based in Kumi, and entered telecommunications hardware, with switchboards as its early products; in the 1980s the company grouped its electronics under Samsung Electronics. After Lee died in 1987 the group was divided into five business groups: Samsung Group, Shinsegae Group, CJ Group, Hansol Group and JoongAng Group.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Samsung Group is a South Korean conglomerate headquartered in Samsung Town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. It is a chaebol, a family-controlled conglomerate, and comprises around 80 companies in fields such as construction, consumer electronics, financial services, shipbuilding and medical services. Key affiliates include Samsung Electronics, Samsung Heavy Industries, Samsung Engineering, Samsung C&amp;T Corporation and Samsung Life Insurance. The group has had a powerful influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>'s economic development, politics, media and culture, and has been a major driving force behind the Miracle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>. Its logo is blue, said to symbolise stability, reliability and corporate social responsibility. The smartphone platform was launched with the Samsung Solstice line in 2008, later developed into the Galaxy line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samsung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Textiles, footwear, seafood and, from the 2010s, electronics assembled by firms such as <b>Samsung</b> drove growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
